\section{Mission \texorpdfstring{$\Delta v$}{Δv} and Trajectory Architecture}
\label{sec:mission-delta-v-trajectory}

\subsection{Overview}
The UAP-01 Uranus Orbiter \& Atmospheric Probe mission requires a
multi-phase interplanetary trajectory incorporating Earth departure,
heliocentric cruise, Uranus approach, probe release, and orbital
insertion. The total provisional mission $\Delta v$ is:
\[
\Delta v_{\text{total}} = 18.5~\mathrm{km/s}.
\]
This chapter formalizes the trajectory architecture, $\Delta v$
budgeting, and dynamical constraints derived from the UUAS multi-scale
simulation framework.

\subsection{Trajectory Phases}
The mission consists of five primary dynamical phases:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Earth departure and escape.
    \item Deep-space cruise with ion propulsion.
    \item Uranus approach and probe release.
    \item Probe entry and descent.
    \item Orbiter insertion and science orbit shaping.
\end{enumerate}

Each phase contributes to the cumulative $\Delta v$ requirement.

\subsection{Earth Departure}
Earth escape velocity from low Earth orbit (LEO) is:
\[
v_{\text{esc}} = \sqrt{2\mu_{\oplus}/r_{\text{LEO}}}
\approx 10.9~\mathrm{km/s}.
\]
The launch vehicle provides the majority of this energy, leaving the
spacecraft with a post-injection hyperbolic excess velocity:
\[
v_{\infty} \approx 3.3~\mathrm{km/s}.
\]

\subsection{Deep-Space Cruise}
Ion propulsion provides continuous low-thrust acceleration over the
heliocentric cruise phase. The cumulative cruise $\Delta v$ is:
\[
\Delta v_{\text{cruise}} = 5.31~\mathrm{km/s},
\]
consistent with the UAP-01 propulsion subsystem hierarchy.

The thrust profile is governed by:
\[
\frac{d\mathbf{v}}{dt} = \frac{T}{m(t)}\hat{\mathbf{t}},
\]
where $T$ is thrust, $m(t)$ spacecraft mass, and $\hat{\mathbf{t}}$ the
thrust direction.

\subsection{Uranus Approach and Probe Release}
The approach trajectory is shaped to align with UUAS MHD-Q1 dynamo
sampling requirements. The probe is released on a pre-entry hyperbolic
trajectory with:
\[
v_{\text{rel}} \approx 1.2~\mathrm{km/s},
\]
ensuring correct atmospheric entry geometry.

\subsection{Probe Entry and Descent}
The atmospheric probe enters Uranus’s atmosphere with entry velocity:
\[
v_{\text{entry}} \approx 22.4~\mathrm{km/s},
\]
dissipated through:
\begin{itemize}
    \item PICA-15 heat shield ablation,
    \item supersonic deceleration,
    \item parachute deployment,
    \item terminal descent stabilization.
\end{itemize}

No propulsive $\Delta v$ is required for the probe.

\subsection{Orbiter Insertion}
Orbital insertion requires:
\[
\Delta v_{\text{insert}} = 10.47~\mathrm{km/s},
\]
matching the spacecraft hierarchy (UAP-06, R1).

Insertion is performed using ion propulsion over multiple periapsis
passes, governed by:
\[
\Delta v = \int_{t_0}^{t_1} \frac{T}{m(t)}\,dt.
\]

\subsection{Science Orbit Shaping}
Science orbit shaping refines:
\begin{itemize}
    \item magnetic field sampling geometry,
    \item auroral ring observation windows,
    \item ionospheric plasma coverage,
    \item probe-relay alignment.
\end{itemize}

UUAS provides real-time dynamical constraints based on MHD-Q1 and
ION-Q4 outputs.

\subsection{Mission \texorpdfstring{$\Delta v$}{Δv} Budget}
The total mission $\Delta v$ is:
\[
\Delta v_{\text{total}}
= v_{\infty}
+ \Delta v_{\text{cruise}}
+ \Delta v_{\text{insert}}
= 3.3 + 5.31 + 10.47
= 18.5~\mathrm{km/s}.
\]

