\subsection{Trajectory Optimization via UUAS}
UUAS provides trajectory optimization constraints:
\begin{itemize}
    \item MHD-Q1 dynamo geometry for magnetic sampling.
    \item THERMO-Q2 mantle stratification for probe descent timing.
    \item ION-Q4 ionospheric plasma cycles for auroral observation windows.
    \item FHE-Q3 encrypted telemetry timing for data relay.
\end{itemize}

The spacecraft trajectory is continuously refined using:
\[
\mathbf{a}(t) = f_{\text{UUAS}}(\mathbf{r}(t), \mathbf{v}(t)),
\]
where $\mathbf{a}(t)$ is the optimal acceleration vector.

\subsection{Conclusion}
The UAP-01 mission requires a total $\Delta v$ of 18.5~km/s, distributed
across Earth departure, deep-space cruise, and Uranus orbital insertion.
Trajectory geometry is tightly coupled to UUAS multi-scale physics
models, ensuring optimal sampling of Uranus’s magnetic field, atmosphere,
ionosphere, and deep interior. The spacecraft and simulation form a
co-evolving dynamical system capable of unprecedented planetary science
fidelity.